\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Generality and mechanism interaction.}
Sign misattribution arises whenever three structural properties co-occur: (a) step-level windowing, (b) sparse binary reward, and (c) group-relative normalization. Under these conditions, the advantage sign collapses to a proxy for episode outcome regardless of local quality, and severity scales with task difficulty ($P(\text{correct} \mid \text{update}) \to p$). M1 and M2 interact in a vicious cycle: M1 lowers $p$ by preventing multi-step strategies, increasing wrong-sign updates under M2, which further degrades $p$---explaining why step-independent training produces active collapse rather than slow convergence.

\paragraph{Why step-independent became the default.}
Six contributing factors---from context length constraints to framework design inertia---are detailed in Appendix~\ref{app:why_defaults}.

\paragraph{Seed-dependent OOD generalization.}
The multi-seed evaluation (Section~\ref{sec:seed_variability}) reveals a bimodal distribution: a stagnant cluster (10--16\% OOD, NS vs.\ SFT) and a generalized cluster (25--31\%, $p{<}0.001$). Training duration does not explain the gap (seed100: $+$1.50pp from step~5 to~10; seed5: 25.37\% at step~10), pointing to early training dynamics or initialization sensitivity. Identifying this mechanism is an important open question.

\paragraph{Practitioner guidance.}
Use episode-level credit when the full episode fits in context; otherwise use the largest feasible $h$ ($h{\geq}20$; $h{\leq}10$ collapses). Finer-grained methods (Turn-PPO, GAGPO) should be applied only after establishing an episode-level baseline, as they assume correct gradient direction.

